        {
          "q": "This question has three parts. A company's weekly profit (in thousands of dollars) from producing $x$ hundred units is modeled by $P(x)=-2x^2+20x-32$. (a) Factor $P(x)$ to find the break-even production levels, where profit is zero. (b) Find the vertex of the parabola to determine the production level that maximizes profit, and state the maximum profit. (c) State the range of production levels (in hundreds of units) for which profit is positive.",
          "answer": "(a) $P(x)=-2(x^2-10x+16)=-2(x-2)(x-8)$. Break-even at $x=2$ and $x=8$ (hundreds of units). (b) Vertex: $x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}=-\\dfrac{20}{2(-2)}=5$. $P(5)=-2(25)+20(5)-32=-50+100-32=18$, so the maximum profit is 18 thousand dollars at $x=5$ hundred units (500 units). (c) Profit is positive between the roots: $2<x<8$, i.e. between 200 and 800 units."
